    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe controller-side logic is coherent and reads well: `assignHousehold` \u2192 `isHouseholdInUse(householdId, \u2026)` \u2192 the `householdInUse && !sharesHousehold` 409 \u2192 the `declaredHouseholdMember` guard on soft-duplicate detection all sit in sensible units, and removing the now-redundant `sameHousehold` helper plus updating the javadoc/OpenAPI docs is the right cleanup. One structural issue, though:\n\n**`assignHousehold` still carries DB-oriented scaffolding it no longer earns** (`ClinicServiceImpl.java:266-270`). Before this change the method fanned out over `ownerRepository.findAll()` and called `ownerRepository.save(existing)` to re-link owners, which is why it was `@Transactional`. After the change the body is a single pure computation:\n\n```java\n@Override\n@Transactional\npublic void assignHousehold(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException {\n    owner.setHouseholdId(householdIdFor(owner.getLastName(), owner.getPostcode()));\n}\n```\n\nIt touches no repository, opens no persistence work, and operates on a still-transient `Owner`. The `@Transactional` annotation is now dead scaffolding left over from the old implementation and misrepresents the method's contract \u2014 a maintainer reading it would reasonably expect DB interaction that isn't there. Drop the `@Transactional` (the uniform `throws DataAccessException` across the `ClinicService` interface can stay for interface consistency).\n\nWorth a second thought (not blocking): since `assignHousehold` is now just a thin mutating wrapper around the private static `householdIdFor`, consider whether \"assign\" is still the right verb for what is now a deterministic derive-and-set, or whether the two-step \"assign then read back `owner.getHouseholdId()`\" in the controller would be clearer as the service returning the computed id. Either is defensible; the `@Transactional` removal is the concrete fix.",
